{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-8516" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $8,$ and that $P$ makes an angle of ${150}^{∘}$ with the positive $x$-axis, what are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992889/state.json" "file_sha256" "1feb7fd7d33f30ddc76682732b16ea97403a8cc70c967f7ee68285038d67166e"} {"question" "q-8516" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ where\n\n$$\nr=8,\\,\\theta ={150}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡({150}^{∘}) \\\\  & =8(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-4\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡({150}^{∘}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-4\\sqrt{3},4).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992889/state.json" "file_sha256" "1feb7fd7d33f30ddc76682732b16ea97403a8cc70c967f7ee68285038d67166e"} {"question" "q-8516" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992889/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ca68a3329f67f46a676a39bf9c28745552eb4f176c91d4a51b375d9a4cd498ac"} {"question" "q-8516" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992889/state.json" "file_sha256" "1feb7fd7d33f30ddc76682732b16ea97403a8cc70c967f7ee68285038d67166e"} {"question" "q-8516" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-4\\sqrt{3},4)"] ["math" "(-4\\sqrt{3},4\\sqrt{2})"] ["math" "(-4\\sqrt{3},8\\sqrt{3})"] ["math" "(4\\sqrt{3},2\\sqrt{3})"] ["math" "(4\\sqrt{3},4)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992889/state.json" "file_sha256" "1feb7fd7d33f30ddc76682732b16ea97403a8cc70c967f7ee68285038d67166e"} {"question" "q-8516" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(-4\\sqrt{3},4)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992889/state.json" "file_sha256" "1feb7fd7d33f30ddc76682732b16ea97403a8cc70c967f7ee68285038d67166e"} {"question" "q-81221" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992889/assets/6e9d2238ca77532c3adda8d35f94061d108c3e15432ce48fdc33885ddf4f4e48.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. If the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(5,\\frac{7\\pi }{6})$ and $m∠AOB=\\frac{5\\pi }{6},$ what are the Cartesian coordinates of $B?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992889/state.json" "file_sha256" "1feb7fd7d33f30ddc76682732b16ea97403a8cc70c967f7ee68285038d67166e"} {"question" "q-81221" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, since $A$ has polar coordinates $(5,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}),$ we must have $r=5.$ Also, since the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius $5,$ the $r$-coordinate of $B$ must also be $5.$\n\nNotice that one must traverse the circle clockwise to move from the point $A$ to point $B.$ Since the $\\theta $-coordinate of $A$ is $\\frac{7\\pi }{6},$ the $\\theta $-coordinate of $B$ must be\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{B}=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(5,\\frac{\\pi }{3})$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992889/state.json" "file_sha256" "1feb7fd7d33f30ddc76682732b16ea97403a8cc70c967f7ee68285038d67166e"} {"question" "q-81221" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992889/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ca68a3329f67f46a676a39bf9c28745552eb4f176c91d4a51b375d9a4cd498ac"} {"question" "q-81221" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992889/state.json" "file_sha256" "1feb7fd7d33f30ddc76682732b16ea97403a8cc70c967f7ee68285038d67166e"} {"question" "q-81221" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(2\\sqrt{2},4)"] ["math" "(2\\sqrt{2},\\frac{9}{2})"] ["math" "(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{9}{2})"] ["math" "(\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2})"] ["math" "(\\sqrt{3},4)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992889/state.json" "file_sha256" "1feb7fd7d33f30ddc76682732b16ea97403a8cc70c967f7ee68285038d67166e"} {"question" "q-81221" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2})"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992889/state.json" "file_sha256" "1feb7fd7d33f30ddc76682732b16ea97403a8cc70c967f7ee68285038d67166e"} {"question" "q-120455" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,\\frac{5\\pi }{6})$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $({r}^{2},\\frac{11\\pi }{6}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(-3,\\sqrt{3}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992889/state.json" "file_sha256" "1feb7fd7d33f30ddc76682732b16ea97403a8cc70c967f7ee68285038d67166e"} {"question" "q-120455" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find $r,$ the distance between $A$ and the origin:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n((\\sqrt{12})^{2},\\frac{11\\pi }{6})=(12,\\frac{11\\pi }{6}).\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =12(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =12(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(6\\sqrt{3},-6).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992889/state.json" "file_sha256" "1feb7fd7d33f30ddc76682732b16ea97403a8cc70c967f7ee68285038d67166e"} {"question" "q-120455" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992889/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ca68a3329f67f46a676a39bf9c28745552eb4f176c91d4a51b375d9a4cd498ac"} {"question" "q-120455" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992889/state.json" "file_sha256" "1feb7fd7d33f30ddc76682732b16ea97403a8cc70c967f7ee68285038d67166e"} {"question" "q-120455" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-\\sqrt{3},-2\\sqrt{3})"] ["math" "(4\\sqrt{3},-\\sqrt{3})"] ["math" "(6,-6)"] ["math" "(6\\sqrt{3},-6)"] ["math" "(\\sqrt{3},-6)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992889/state.json" "file_sha256" "1feb7fd7d33f30ddc76682732b16ea97403a8cc70c967f7ee68285038d67166e"} {"question" "q-120455" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(6\\sqrt{3},-6)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992889/state.json" "file_sha256" "1feb7fd7d33f30ddc76682732b16ea97403a8cc70c967f7ee68285038d67166e"} {"question" "q-120593" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992889/assets/48517c5d3fa340bed1cc7b5493f0099a72afa79d9e0690b79695afe018c8a05c.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. If the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(6,\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ and $m∠AOB=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what are the Cartesian coordinates of $B?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992889/state.json" "file_sha256" "1feb7fd7d33f30ddc76682732b16ea97403a8cc70c967f7ee68285038d67166e"} {"question" "q-120593" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, since $A$ has polar coordinates $(6,\\frac{\\pi }{6}),$ we must have $r=6.$ Also, since the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius $6,$ the $r$-coordinate of $B$ must also be $6.$ \n\nNotice that one must traverse the circle counterclockwise to move from the point $A$ to point $B.$ Since the $\\theta $-coordinate of $A$ is $\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ the $\\theta $-coordinate of $B$ must be\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{B}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(6,\\frac{2\\pi }{3})$ \n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =6(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =6(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-3,3\\sqrt{3}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992889/state.json" "file_sha256" "1feb7fd7d33f30ddc76682732b16ea97403a8cc70c967f7ee68285038d67166e"} {"question" "q-120593" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992889/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ca68a3329f67f46a676a39bf9c28745552eb4f176c91d4a51b375d9a4cd498ac"} {"question" "q-120593" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992889/state.json" "file_sha256" "1feb7fd7d33f30ddc76682732b16ea97403a8cc70c967f7ee68285038d67166e"} {"question" "q-120593" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-3,3)"] ["math" "(-3,3\\sqrt{3})"] ["math" "(-3\\sqrt{2},3)"] ["math" "(-3\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{3})"] ["math" "(\\sqrt{2},3\\sqrt{3})"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992889/state.json" "file_sha256" "1feb7fd7d33f30ddc76682732b16ea97403a8cc70c967f7ee68285038d67166e"} {"question" "q-120593" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(-3,3\\sqrt{3})"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992889/state.json" "file_sha256" "1feb7fd7d33f30ddc76682732b16ea97403a8cc70c967f7ee68285038d67166e"} {"question" "q-276798" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $3,$ and that $P$ makes an angle of ${240}^{∘}$ with the positive $x$-axis, then the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are$\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992889/state.json" "file_sha256" "1feb7fd7d33f30ddc76682732b16ea97403a8cc70c967f7ee68285038d67166e"} {"question" "q-276798" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$, where\n\n$$\nr=3,\\,\\theta ={240}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡({240}^{∘}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡({240}^{∘}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-\\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992889/state.json" "file_sha256" "1feb7fd7d33f30ddc76682732b16ea97403a8cc70c967f7ee68285038d67166e"} {"question" "q-276798" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992889/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ca68a3329f67f46a676a39bf9c28745552eb4f176c91d4a51b375d9a4cd498ac"} {"question" "q-276798" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992889/state.json" "file_sha256" "1feb7fd7d33f30ddc76682732b16ea97403a8cc70c967f7ee68285038d